  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 308 AM CDT Tue Aug 4 2026\n\nCOOLER AND LESS HUMID NEXT FEW DAYS...The main weather feature of \ninterest is a cold front advancing eastward across the area early \nthis morning. The front has produced scattered showers and isolated \nthunderstorms across western and central MN. Occasional wind gusts \nbetween 35-45mph have been observed with the frontal passage as \nwinds shift out of the northwest. As expected, the broken line of \nshowers and thunderstorms has weakened with eastward movement, which \nis forecast to continue this morning. We'll keep scattered shower \nchances around through the first couple of hours after daybreak \nacross southeastern MN and western WI. A chance of showers remains \nalong the MN/IA border this afternoon as the front moves south.\nCooler, more comfortable air arrives as a region of high pressure \nand drier weather builds into the Upper Midwest. Afternoon highs run \nbelow normal, in the 70s, the next couple of days.\n\nWARMING UP LATER THIS WEEK INTO THE WEEKEND...Beginning Thursday \nhighs will return to the lower 80s and continue warming through the \nweekend. However, the degree of warmth is somewhat uncertain due to \nthe upper-level pattern. By Friday, the Upper Midwest will be \npositioned within a channel of enhanced northwesterly flow aloft, \nlocated between an upper-low over central Canada and an expansive \nridge over the desert southwest. Guidance brings a cold front \nthrough the region Friday, which may produce scattered precipitation \nchances. As the weekend progresses, the northwesterly flow pattern \nwill become more westerly/zonal around the ridge, which may support \nadditional precipitation chances, though confidence in timing and \ncoverage is low at this time. This is because there are differences \nacross the ensemble membership with regards to the flattening of the \nridge and northward expansion of mid-level warm air over the \nweekend. For now, we'll roll with highs in the mid 80s Saturday & \nSunday.",
